		{"id":"381abb22b44c8040","x":-408,"y":-178,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"```text\n<!-- ## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe **Chart of Accounts** is a master/reference file that serves as the foundational structure for categorizing financial accounts within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It defines the enterprise's financial structure by classifying all financial activities into consistent, reportable categories, ensuring that each account is unique and correctly typed (asset, liability, equity, revenue, expense). This table is essential for establishing account hierarchies and supports the validation of financial transactions recorded in the General Ledger and Trial Balance.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 455\n\n- Unique account names: 455\n\n- Leaf accounts under Assets: 87 (64.4% of total assets are current)\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **dim**: Represents the dimension type for financial accounts, categorizing account types within the Chart of Accounts.\n\n- **member_name**: Identifies the names of financial accounts, crucial for recognizing account categories.\n\n- **parent_name**: Establishes the parent categories of financial accounts, essential for defining account hierarchies.\n\n- **operator**: Indicates the mathematical operator used for account calculations, defining relationships between accounts.\n\n- **member_alias**: Provides alternative identifiers for financial accounts, useful for referencing accounts in various contexts.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- **cmd**: 0% populated, entirely null\n\n- **member_id**: 0% populated, entirely null\n\n- **external_id**: 0% populated, entirely null\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- All expected critical fields for this file type are present per FP&A standards.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe Chart of Accounts is structured hierarchically, with major branches representing different categories of accounts:\n\n- **Balance Sheet**: Contains assets, liabilities, and equity accounts.\n\n- **Assets**: 87 leaf accounts, including current and non-current assets.\n\n- **Liabilities**: 32 leaf accounts, including current liabilities.\n\n- **Equity**: Accounts related to retained earnings and common stock.\n\n- **Revenue**: Includes operating revenue accounts.\n\n- **Expenses**: Comprises various operating expenses.\n\n  \n\nThe hierarchy allows for a balance check where total assets equal total liabilities plus equity, ensuring clarity in financial reporting.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The primary key is **member_name**, which uniquely identifies each account.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**:\n\n- The Chart of Accounts is referenced by the General Ledger entries via **account_number**, establishing a many-to-one relationship.\n\n- The Trial Balance validates accounts from the Chart of Accounts, also establishing a many-to-one relationship.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: Account codes primarily range from 1001 to 1730, with specific leaf accounts under each category.\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Account names follow a structured format, often descriptive of their financial function (e.g., \"Current Assets\", \"Accounts Payable\").\n\n- **Operator/flag usage**: The operator column predominantly contains \"+\" for revenue accounts, indicating positive contributions to net income.\n\n- **Value distributions**: The dataset shows a concentration of accounts within the specified ranges, with 64.4% of assets classified as current.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**:\n\n- Critical fields such as **member_name**, **dim**, **parent_name**, and **operator** are fully populated (100%).\n\n- Unpopulated fields like **cmd**, **member_id**, and **external_id** are entirely null, which may limit cross-system integration and operational control.\n\n- **Consistency**: The naming patterns and account structures adhere to expected accounting norms, with no deviations noted.\n\n- **Gaps**: The presence of entirely null fields indicates potential areas for improvement, particularly in operational control and traceability.\n\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified, ensuring the integrity of the account structure.\n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**: The data conforms to standard accounting practices, supporting the necessary financial reporting and analysis functions. -->\n\n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n<!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->\n\n  \n  \n\n## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe **Chart of Accounts** (COA) serves as a **hierarchical structure** for categorizing financial accounts within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It is essential for organizing financial data, ensuring that all accounts are unique, correctly typed (asset, liability, equity, revenue, expense), and aligned with reporting hierarchies. This table contains the necessary structure to support financial reporting and validation processes.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 455\n\n- Unique account identifiers: 455 (each account is distinct)\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **member_name**: Represents the names of financial accounts, used for identifying account categories within the Chart of Accounts.\n\n- **parent_name**: Indicates the parent categories of financial accounts, establishing account hierarchies essential for financial reporting.\n\n- **operator**: Defines the mathematical operator used for account calculations, indicating whether accounts contribute positively or negatively to net income.\n\n- **member_alias**: Provides alternative identifiers for financial accounts, facilitating references in various contexts.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- **cmd**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n- **member_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n- **external_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- All expected critical fields for this file type are present per FP&A standards.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe Chart of Accounts is organized hierarchically, with major branches representing different categories of accounts:\n\n  \n\n- **Balance Sheet** (1xxx)\n\n- **Assets (+)** (87 leaf accounts)\n\n- **Current Assets** (56 leaf accounts)\n\n- **Non-Current Assets** (31 leaf accounts)\n\n- **Liabilities** (2xxx)\n\n- **Current Liabilities (-)** (32 leaf accounts)\n\n- **Equity** (3xxx)\n\n- **Revenue** (4xxx)\n\n- **Expenses** (5xxx)\n\n- **Other Income/Expenses** (6xxx)\n\n  \n\nThe **operator** field indicates the sign logic for accounts, where revenue accounts contribute positively and expense accounts negatively to net income.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The primary key is **member_name**, which uniquely identifies each account in the Chart of Accounts.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**:\n\n- The Chart of Accounts is referenced by the **General Ledger** through the **account_number**, establishing a many-to-one relationship for tracking financial transactions.\n\n- It is also validated by the **Trial Balance**, ensuring accounting integrity through another many-to-one relationship.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: Account codes primarily range from **1001 to 1730**, with specific accounts like **Bank** and **Trade Payables**.\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Account names are structured with clear categories (e.g., **Assets**, **Liabilities**) and often include numeric identifiers.\n\n- **Operator/flag usage**: The **operator** field predominantly uses \"+\" for revenue accounts, indicating their positive contribution to net income.\n\n- **Value distributions**: The dataset includes **87 leaf accounts** under Assets, with **64.4%** classified as Current Assets.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**:\n\n- **member_name**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n\n- **parent_name**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n\n- **operator**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n\n- **member_alias**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n\n- **Consistency**:\n\n- Account names follow a structured format, with unique identifiers for each account.\n\n- **Gaps**:\n\n- Missing fields like **cmd**, **member_id**, and **external_id** do not impact the core functionality of the Chart of Accounts as they are not critical for its primary purpose.\n\n- **Integrity issues**:\n\n- No duplicates found in **member_name** (455 unique values).\n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n\n- The structure adheres to financial reporting standards, ensuring that accounts are uniquely identified and correctly categorized for reporting and validation purposes.\n```"},
		{"id":"6729610d8b96b048","x":-123,"y":-160,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"```\n<!-- ## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe table identified as the **General Ledger** serves as a **Transactional** file type within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It is designed to record detailed financial transactions, ensuring that each entry is traceable and compliant with accounting standards. The structure includes essential elements such as unique identifiers for each transaction, account numbers, and monetary amounts, which are critical for maintaining the integrity of financial reporting.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 24,675\n\n- Unique account numbers: 307\n\n- Unique document numbers: 2,984\n\n- Unique line items: 24,675\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each financial account, essential for tracking transactions.\n\n- **amount**: Monetary value associated with transactions, critical for financial analysis.\n\n- **line_unique_key**: Unique key for each line item, ensuring traceability and audit readiness.\n\n- **document_number**: Unique identifier for documents associated with transactions, used for tracking.\n\n- **date**: Transaction date for financial entries, necessary for temporal tracking of transactions.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- None identified; all columns are populated.\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- **debit_amount and credit_amount**: Required per double-entry accounting standards to validate transaction balance and ensure accounting integrity. The current structure does not distinguish between debits and credits, which prevents automated balance validation.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe General Ledger table is structured as a flat file with each row representing a unique financial transaction. The primary key, **line_unique_key**, ensures that each entry is distinct. The relationships to the **Chart of Accounts** and **Trial Balance** are established through the **account_number**, which links transactions to their respective accounts for reporting and validation purposes.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **line_unique_key** serves as the primary key, linking to the **Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format** and **Trial Balance** through the **account_number**.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **account_number** must reference valid accounts in the Trial Balance to ensure accurate financial reporting and integrity.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** ranges from 1011.0 to 2021.0, indicating a structured numbering system.\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Document numbers follow a consistent format, with both numeric and alphanumeric identifiers.\n\n- **Value distributions**: The **amount** field shows a wide range of values, with unique amounts totaling 14,681, indicating diverse transaction types.\n\n- **Grouping patterns**: Transactions are categorized by **account_number**, allowing for easy aggregation and reporting.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**: All critical fields are fully populated, with no null values present.\n\n- **Consistency**: The data adheres to expected naming conventions and formats, with no deviations noted.\n\n- **Gaps**: The absence of separate debit and credit fields is a significant gap, impacting the ability to validate transaction balances.\n\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified, ensuring the integrity of the data.\n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**: The data generally conforms to GAAP and IFRS standards, although the lack of distinct debit and credit fields is a notable compliance issue that could affect financial reporting accuracy. -->\n\n  \n  \n  \n\n<!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->\n\n  \n  \n  \n\n## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe **General Ledger** table serves as a **Transactional file** within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It captures detailed financial transactions, ensuring that all entries are linked to unique accounts defined in the Chart of Accounts. This structure is essential for tracking financial activities, validating accounting integrity, and supporting accurate financial reporting.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 24,675\n\n- Unique account numbers: 307\n\n- Unique document numbers: 2,984\n\n- Unique invoice numbers: 2,988\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each financial account, essential for tracking transactions.\n\n- **amount**: Monetary value associated with transactions, critical for financial analysis.\n\n- **document_number**: Unique identifier for documents linked to each transaction, used for tracking and referencing.\n\n- **line_unique_key**: Unique key for each line item, ensuring traceability and audit readiness of financial transactions.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- None identified; all columns are populated.\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- **debit_amount and credit_amount**: Required per double-entry accounting standards to validate transaction balance and ensure accounting integrity. The current single 'amount' field does not distinguish between debit and credit, preventing automated balance validation.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe General Ledger table is structured as a flat file containing detailed records of financial transactions. Each entry is linked to a unique account through the `account_number`, which is essential for maintaining the integrity of financial reporting. The `line_unique_key` ensures that each transaction can be traced back to its source, supporting audit readiness.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The `line_unique_key` serves as the primary key, linking to the Chart of Accounts and Trial Balance tables. This relationship ensures that all transactions are validated against the defined accounts.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The `account_number` must reference valid accounts in the Trial Balance, ensuring accurate financial reporting and integrity.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The `account_number` ranges from 1011 to 2021, with a total of 307 unique identifiers.\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Document numbers follow a consistent format, with both numeric and alphanumeric identifiers (e.g., \"Journal #83171\").\n\n- **Value distributions**: The `amount` field shows a diverse range of values, with 14,681 unique entries, indicating varied transaction sizes.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**:\n\n- `account_number`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n\n- `amount`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n\n- `document_number`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n\n- `line_unique_key`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n\n  \n\n- **Consistency**:\n\n- `account_number` follows a consistent format with a mix of numeric values.\n\n- `document_number` is consistent, with a mix of numeric and alphanumeric formats.\n\n  \n\n- **Gaps**:\n\n- The absence of distinct debit and credit fields prevents automated validation of transaction balances, which is critical for maintaining accounting integrity.\n\n  \n\n- **Integrity issues**:\n\n- No duplicates found in `line_unique_key` (24,675 unique values).\n\n- All `account_number` references are valid, ensuring traceability.\n\n  \n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n\n- The single `amount` field without a debit/credit distinction violates double-entry accounting principles, which require separate tracking of debits and credits for accurate financial reporting.\n```"},
		{"id":"bc4bf049bfafb16f","x":144,"y":-162,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"```\n<!-- ## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe **Trial Balance** is a critical financial reporting tool used in Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) to validate accounting integrity by ensuring that total debits equal total credits for a specific period and entity. This table serves as a master file that consolidates financial data from various accounts, allowing for accurate financial reporting and analysis.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 348\n\n- Unique accounts: 132 (account_number)\n\n- Unique subsidiaries: 6 (subsidiary)\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each account in the Chart of Accounts, essential for financial reporting and transaction tracking.\n\n- **subsidiary**: Name of the subsidiary associated with each account, used for financial reporting and analysis by subsidiary.\n\n- **subsidiary_id**: Unique identifier for each subsidiary, linking accounts to their respective subsidiaries.\n\n- **formula_numeric**: Represents calculated financial figures for accounts, used for financial reporting and performance analysis.\n\n- **last_month**: Indicates the month and year for the last reporting period, facilitating historical financial comparison.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- **opponent**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n- **day**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- **debit_amount and credit_amount**: Required per double-entry accounting standards to validate transaction balance and ensure accounting integrity. The absence of these fields prevents automated balance validation and could lead to misreporting of financial positions.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe Trial Balance is structured as a flat file that consolidates financial data across various accounts and subsidiaries. Each record corresponds to a unique account, with associated financial figures for the reporting period. The primary key is the **account_number**, which links to the Chart of Accounts, ensuring that all accounts are validated against a standardized structure.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **account_number** serves as a primary key that references the Chart of Accounts, ensuring that all accounts are validated for integrity.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **subsidiary_id** links to the subsidiaries table, establishing a relationship that allows for detailed financial reporting by subsidiary.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** ranges from 1000 to 2000, with examples including \"1036.0\", \"2014.0\", and \"1715.0\".\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Subsidiary names follow a consistent format, e.g., \"Subsidiary 1\", \"Subsidiary 2\".\n\n- **Value distributions**: The **formula_numeric** field shows a range of calculated values, with some accounts reflecting significant negative balances, indicating potential losses or adjustments.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**:\n\n- **account_number**: 100% populated\n\n- **subsidiary**: 100% populated\n\n- **subsidiary_id**: 100% populated\n\n- **formula_numeric**: 100% populated\n\n- **last_month**: 100% populated\n\n- **opponent**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n- **day**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\nThe absence of critical fields like debit and credit amounts significantly impacts the table's ability to serve its purpose in validating financial integrity.\n\n  \n\n- **Consistency**: The naming conventions for subsidiaries and account numbers are consistent, adhering to expected formats. However, the lack of debit and credit fields deviates from standard accounting practices.\n\n  \n\n- **Gaps**: The missing debit and credit fields hinder the table's compliance with double-entry accounting standards, which is essential for accurate financial reporting.\n\n  \n\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified, but the absence of critical fields raises concerns about the table's overall integrity and reliability for financial analysis.\n\n  \n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**: The data structure aligns with GAAP and IFRS standards, but the missing critical fields prevent full compliance with accounting principles necessary for robust financial reporting.\n\n-->\n\n  \n  \n  \n\n<!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->\n\n  \n  \n  \n\n## What this table is\n\n  \n\nThe **Trial Balance** is a critical **analytical/reporting file** used in Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) to validate accounting integrity by ensuring that total debits equal total credits for a specific period and entity. This table serves as a foundational control layer in the financial reporting process, ensuring that all financial transactions are accurately captured and reported.\n\n  \n\n**Size/Scope:**\n\n- Total rows: 348\n\n- Contains 16 columns, including key identifiers and financial figures.\n\n  \n\n## Column definitions\n\n  \n\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each account in the Chart of Accounts, essential for tracking financial transactions and reporting.\n\n- **formula_numeric**: Represents calculated financial figures for accounts, used for performance analysis and reporting.\n\n- **subsidiary**: Name of the subsidiary associated with each account, important for financial reporting by subsidiary.\n\n- **subsidiary_id**: Unique identifier for each subsidiary, used for linking accounts to their respective subsidiaries.\n\n  \n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n  \n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n\n- **opponent**: Not populated (0% populated).\n\n- **day**: Not populated (0% populated).\n\n  \n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n\n- **debit_balance and credit_balance**: Required to classify account balances and validate that total debits equal total credits per accounting equation. The current structure lacks these distinctions, preventing automated validation of the trial balance.\n\n- **account_type/normal_balance_indicator**: Necessary to determine the nature of account balances (debit or credit). Without this, it is impossible to ascertain the validity of the trial balance.\n\n  \n\n## The structure it defines\n\n  \n\nThe Trial Balance is structured as a flat file that aggregates financial data by account. Each row corresponds to a unique account identified by the **account_number**, with associated financial figures for various periods. The relationships to the **Chart of Accounts** and **General Ledger** are established through the **account_number**, ensuring that all accounts are validated against the defined financial structure.\n\n  \n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n  \n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **account_number** serves as the primary key, linking to the **Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format** to validate accounts.\n\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **subsidiary_id** links to the **General Ledger**, ensuring that financial transactions are validated against the correct subsidiary.\n\n  \n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n  \n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** values range from 1005.0 to 2014.0, indicating a structured numbering system.\n\n- **Naming conventions**: Subsidiary names follow a consistent format (e.g., \"Subsidiary 1\", \"Subsidiary 2\").\n\n- **Value distributions**: The **formula_numeric** field shows a mix of positive and negative values, indicating various financial outcomes across accounts.\n\n  \n\n## Quality of the data\n\n  \n\n- **Completeness**:\n\n- **account_number**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n\n- **formula_numeric**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n\n- **subsidiary**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n\n- **subsidiary_id**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n\n- **Consistency**:\n\n- The **account_number** follows a consistent format, with no duplicates found (132 unique values).\n\n- The **formula_numeric** values show a range of financial figures, indicating active financial reporting.\n\n  \n\n- **Gaps**:\n\n- Missing **debit_balance and credit_balance** prevents automated validation of the trial balance.\n\n- Absence of **account_type/normal_balance_indicator** limits the ability to assess account balance nature.\n\n  \n\n- **Integrity issues**:\n\n- No duplicates found in **account_number** (348 unique values).\n\n- All **subsidiary_id** references are valid, ensuring integrity in subsidiary reporting.\n\n  \n\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n\n- The lack of debit/credit balance fields violates double-entry accounting principles, which are essential for maintaining accounting integrity. The current structure does not allow for the validation of the accounting equation (total debits = total credits).\n```"}
